I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 8 of 943 (part 7 of 7).\par\par\par
The Tail: What It Still Costs Me At Breakfast\par\par\par
It's been four months and my wife still glances at my phone when I check it in the morning.\par\par\par
It's been four months and my wife still glances at my phone when I check it in the morning.\par\par\par
She doesn't say anything. She doesn't ask. She just glances. A little flick of the eyes, off her coffee cup, over to my hand, and back. It takes maybe half a second. She's not doing it to hurt me. She's doing it because for eight days in October I lied to her at breakfast about what I was doing on the phone, and now her nervous system files 'phone in the morning' under 'thing to keep an eye on.'\par\par\par
That's the tail. That's what it still costs me. Not the money. The money's gone and mostly forgotten. The mortgage got paid. The credit card got paid. We moved on financially. What we did not move on from is the half-second glance.\par\par\par
I thought about hiding this post. I thought about not writing it. Because in the arc of a good confession you're supposed to end with something. A lesson. A shape. A little rise. And what I have is a woman glancing at a phone.\par\par\par
Here are the other things it still costs me.\par\par\par
I don't watch football with the sound on anymore. Not because of the football. Because of the commercials. Every third ad is a sportsbook. Every third ad has some guy in a sharp suit saying 'lock' or 'edge' or 'sharp' and my whole body does a little thing. Not a big thing. A little thing. The way a dog does when the doorbell rings on TV.\par\par\par
My brother-in-law Dane still tries to talk to me about prediction markets at family gatherings. I told him once, at a Sunday dinner at his house, that his 'sharp' had cost me $300 and had no P&L to speak of. He got red. He hasn't fully forgiven me. His wife told my wife that I embarrassed him. My wife told her sister that maybe Dane needed embarrassing. That created something between the sisters that hasn't quite healed. So there's a wedge in a family, running through a Sunday dinner, that started with a tweet by a man I have never met.\par\par\par
I still, sometimes, at maybe 10pm on a bored Tuesday, feel the pull. I don't act on it. But feeling it is its own small tax. There's a version of my brain that will never fully believe I don't have edge. That version of my brain has been quieted, not killed. It sits in the back of the room. It raises its hand occasionally.\par\par\par
I look at my phone less. This sounds like a benefit but it isn't, entirely. I look at my phone less because I don't fully trust myself with it. I have removed apps I used to enjoy because they were adjacent to the ones that hurt me. I no longer read certain writers I used to like because their audiences overlap with gambling audiences and I don't want the recommendations. My internet has gotten smaller. Some of that is good. Some of it is just smaller.\par\par\par
My spiral notebook is still in the drawer under the toaster oven. I don't need it there. It could go anywhere. But it lives there because that's where I put it in October and moving it feels like declaring victory, and I am not declaring victory. I am, at 55, four months in, cautiously optimistic that I have learned something. I have been cautiously optimistic before. The notebook stays in the drawer.\par\par\par
Here's the one I didn't want to write. My wife, three weeks ago, was talking about a trip we might take next summer. Somewhere with a beach. She mentioned a number. A budget. I said sure, that sounds fine. And she said, and this is exact, 'We could actually afford a little more but I want to keep a cushion.' And I knew, in the way you know when you've hurt someone in a way that will not fully unhurt, that the cushion existed because of me. That we were budgeting the beach around the possibility that I might, at some point, in some quiet moment, do it again.\par\par\par
She didn't say that. She might not even have meant that. But that's what I heard. And that's what I'll hear for a while.\par\par\par
The cost of $1,528 in Kalshi losses is not $1,528. The cost is: a smaller trip in the summer. A glance at a phone at breakfast. A brother-in-law who won't quite look at me. A sister-in-law who won't quite look at my wife. A whole subset of the internet I don't visit. A pull at 10pm on a Tuesday. A notebook in a drawer.\par\par\par
And the guy with the anime profile picture is still tweeting. I don't read him anymore. I blocked him in November. But I know, because sometimes I hear other people quote him, that he is still there, still calling contracts mispriced, still posting frog memes at 6am when they crater.\par\par\par
He has 6,100 followers now. He's grown. Good for him. Whatever.\par\par\par
That's the tail.\par\par\par
That's the end of this one. There are nine hundred and forty two more.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
